(*
expect:
every candidate rejected true
nothing was sent true
measurement valid true
fit cost is size-independent true
*)
(* rete#161 fix (1): DECIDING A VALUE DOES NOT FIT MUST NOT COST WHAT ENCODING IT
   COSTS. `valueFits:` used to answer `(Cbor new encode: v) byteSize <= 256` — it
   built every candidate's bytes in a ByteBuilder, read the length, and dropped
   them. It now asks `Cbor sizeOf:`, which runs the same walk into a counting sink.

   WHY THIS IS THE ARENA'S PROBLEM AND NOT A SPEED ONE. A ByteBuilder doubles as it
   grows and `frozenBytes` then takes an exact-size copy, so one encode leaves a
   ladder of abandoned buffers whose top rung is the size of the value. rete#199 is
   what that does to a node: pushes refused, `acked` frozen, everything re-collected
   every check-in, until the arena could not find 4104 contiguous bytes and the node
   halted with 34% of it live. The rows it died measuring were rows it was never
   going to send.

   THE TEST IS THAT MEASURED SHAPE, INVERTED INTO A PROPERTY. Every candidate here
   is over `maxValueBytes`, so all of them are rejected, `collectOutbound` answers
   empty and `pushFrame` returns nil BEFORE the frame encode — the arena delta over
   one `pushFrame` is therefore the collect and the fit test and nothing else. Run
   it twice, once with 303 B values and once with 2003 B values, and the question
   "does deciding cost what encoding costs" is answered by whether the two deltas
   are the same number.

   THEY ARE THE SAME NUMBER, not merely close, and that is why this asserts equality
   rather than a threshold. `sizeOf:` allocates one counting sink per call whatever
   the value is; the paths, the row count and the scan are identical between the two
   legs by construction. A threshold would have to be invented, and rete's standing
   rule is not to invent a tunable before there is data to choose it from — here
   there is nothing to choose, because the honest claim is exact.

   WHAT IT WAS WHEN IT FAILED: 19624 B for the 303 B values and 72104 B for the
   2003 B values, one `pushFrame` each. The larger number is more than a C6's whole
   53 KB arena, spent per check-in, to conclude that nothing could be sent.

   MEASUREMENT VALIDITY IS ASSERTED, NOT ASSUMED. `liveBytes` is a live-set gauge,
   so a delta across the window equals the bytes allocated in it ONLY if no
   collection ran — a GC inside the window silently reports a small delta and would
   turn this test green by erasing the very evidence it reads. `gcCycles` is
   therefore read on both sides of every window and pinned unchanged. *)
class FitCostIsSizeIndependent packageUsing: manifest = () (

  base = ( ^ 'abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz0123456789abcdefghijklmn' )

  (* n copies of the 50 B base. 6 -> 303 B encoded, 40 -> 2003 B: both over the
     256 B `maxValueBytes` cap, so both legs reject all eight. *)
  rep: n = (
    | s i |
    s:: ''.
    i:: 1.
    [ i <= n ] whileTrue: [ s:: s , self base. i:: i + 1 ].
    ^ s )

  (* One leg. Answers a List: allocated bytes, gcCycles delta, rejected count, and
     whether the frame was nil. A fresh space and bridge per leg, so neither leg
     inherits the other's cursor or its `rejected`. *)
  legOn: platform value: v = (
    | space bridge spans g0 g1 l0 l1 frame |
    space:: platform space.
    spans:: platform Spans new.
    bridge:: platform PortaBridge socket: nil space: space id: 'n1' peer: '::1' port: 6969 spans: spans.
    1 to: 8 do: [ :i | space put: ('obs/c' , i printString) value: v ].

    (* A first push, unmeasured: it pays the one-time lazy work (span slots, the
       channel handles the scan memoises) that is not a per-candidate cost, and
       measuring it would hide the per-candidate cost underneath it. Cells are
       re-scanned every check-in until acked, and nothing acks here, so the second
       push sees exactly the same eight candidates. *)
    frame:: bridge pushFrame.

    g0:: platform heapStats gcCycles.
    l0:: platform heapStats liveBytes.
    bridge pushFrame.
    l1:: platform heapStats liveBytes.
    g1:: platform heapStats gcCycles.

    ^ platform List with: l1 - l0 with: g1 - g0 with: bridge rejected with: frame == nil )

  public main: platform args: args = (
    | small large |
    small:: self legOn: platform value: (self rep: 6).
    large:: self legOn: platform value: (self rep: 40).

    (* Eight per push, two pushes per leg: sixteen. If this is not 16 the values
       were not oversize and the legs measured the ordinary send path instead. *)
    ('every candidate rejected ' ,
      (((small at: 3) = 16) and: [ (large at: 3) = 16 ]) printString) println.

    (* Nil frame = the early return fired, so no frame encode is inside either
       window. Without this the deltas would include an encode that legitimately
       scales with the value, and the comparison below would mean nothing. *)
    ('nothing was sent ' ,
      (((small at: 4)) and: [ large at: 4 ]) printString) println.

    ('measurement valid ' ,
      (((small at: 2) = 0) and: [ (large at: 2) = 0 ]) printString) println.

    ('fit cost is size-independent ' , ((small at: 1) = (large at: 1)) printString) println.
    ((small at: 1) = (large at: 1)) ifFalse: [
      ('  small ' , (small at: 1) printString ,
       ' B vs large ' , (large at: 1) printString , ' B') println ].
  )
) : (
)
